Day 246/250: Minimum Absolute Difference

Problem Statement #1200

Problem Statement Explanation:

Given an array of distinct integers arr, find all pairs of elements with the minimum absolute difference of any two elements.
Return a list of pairs in ascending order (with respect to pairs), each pair [a, b] follows:

a, b are from arr a < b
b - a equals to the minimum absolute difference of any two elements in arr

Solution Approach:

To solve this efficiently, we avoid the O(n^2) brute-force approach (comparing every pair) and instead use a Sort & Scan strategy:
Sort the Array: By sorting the array in ascending order, we guarantee that the minimum absolute difference must exist between two adjacent elements.
Single Pass Iteration: * We traverse the sorted array once.
For each adjacent pair (arr[i],arr[i+1]), we calculate the difference.

Case 1: If the current difference is smaller than our known minDiff, we clear our results list, update minDiff, and add the new pair.
Case 2: If the difference matches our minDiff, we simply add the pair to the list.

Here's the code:

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> minimumAbsDifference(int[] arr) {
        Arrays.sort(arr);
        
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        int minDiff = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length - 1; i++) {
            int currentDiff = arr[i + 1] - arr[i];
            
            if (currentDiff < minDiff) {
                minDiff = currentDiff;
                result.clear();
                result.add(Arrays.asList(arr[i], arr[i + 1]));
            } else if (currentDiff == minDiff) {
                result.add(Arrays.asList(arr[i], arr[i + 1]));
            }
        }
        
        return result;
    }
}

Complexity:

Time Complexity: O(nlogn)	
Space Complexity:	O(n)
